<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.2.5: Writing Equations to Represent T-Shirts</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00080</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.2.5: Writing Equations to Represent T-Shirts</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ea80c190-929b-4ddb-8faf-f8d3aad6b6d2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para>A school choir needs to make T-shirts for its 75 members and has set aside some money in their budget to pay for
  them. The members of the choir decided to order from a printing company that charges $3 per shirt, plus a $50 fee for
  each color to be printed on the shirts.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="1" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write an equation that represents the relationship between the number of T-shirts ordered, the number of colors on
    the shirts, and the total cost of the order. If you use a <emphasis>variable</emphasis>, specify what it represents.
  </item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>ompare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>\( 75 \cdot 3 + 50𝑥 = 𝐷 \) (or equivalent) </para>
    <para>\( 𝑥 \) represents the number of colors on the shirts. </para>
    <para>\( D \) represents the total cost in dollars.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> In this situation, which quantities do you think can vary? Which might be fixed?</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>The cost per shirt and the fee per color are fixed (they are set by the printing company). The number of colors
      on the shirts can vary, and so can the total cost, depending on the number of colors being printed.</para>
  </solution></exercise></section>
